  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1204 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nThe latest upper-air observations showcase the weakening of the \nbroad upper-level ridge that has dictated much of the region's \nweather as of late. As this ridge slides toward the Eastern \nSeaboard, it is projected to continue weakening with zonal upper-\nlevel flow being established in its wake. A vortmax near the \nManitoba/Ontario border leads a cyclone between the Northwest \nTerritories and Nunavut that is expected to deepen over the coming \ndays. \n\nFurther south and closer to home, a stationary boundary lingers \nacross northern Missouri, extending southwestward into south-central \nKansas. A shortwave trough helped kick off convection in \nsouthwestern and west-central Kansas earlier Saturday evening. CAMs \nhave struggled to determine whether or not additional initiation \nwill occur in the vicinity of the boundary in northeast Kansas and \nnorthern Missouri during the overnight hours. It does appear that \ninitiation would once again be largely driven by the low-level jet. \nHowever, in comparison to previous nights, less intensification of \nthe low-level jet is expected, peaking at around 25 kts. Some \nupdrafts have developed along the I-70 corridor in west-central \nKansas, perhaps on the nose of a weak low-level jet in the vicinity \nof the stationary boundary. In addition to northern Missouri, this \nwill be an area to watch heading into the overnight. MLCAPE across \nthe region is roughly between 1000-1500 J/kg, but this is \naccompanied by only 20-25 kts of deep layer shear. As such, the \nsevere threat for any overnight convection is mainly limited to \ndownburst winds caused by collapsing updrafts. Heavy rainfall is \nanother concern, with PWATs between 2.0\" and 2.2\". CAMs have hinted \nat training storms in the vicinity of the US-36 corridor...if this \ncomes to fruition, flooding may become a concern. \n\nThe aforementioned shortwave which supported convection in \nsouthwestern and west-central Kansas is expected to move to the east-\nnortheast overnight. There are questions about whether associated \nconvection will be able to sustain itself this through the early \nmorning hours. Previously, CAMs wanted to bring a mature MCS into \nnorthern Kansas and Missouri but are beginning to stray from this \nsolution, likely due to the poor shear locally. Current thinking is \nthat these areas may see some decaying remnants as opposed to a \nstrong complex of storms. \n\nCooler temperatures return to the region Sunday and Monday behind a \ncold front progged to move through Sunday morning. The pace of this \nfront will determine how warm or \"cool\" certain portions of the CWA \nwill be. Confidence is high that maximum temperatures north of the \nMissouri River will be in the low/mid 80s...it is south from here \nthat there is greater uncertainty. Current thinking is that the far \nsouthern fringes of the CWA could range anywhere from the upper 80s \ninto the lower 90s. While certainly not as hot as it has been, these \nareas may need to wait until Monday to feel the same amount of \nrelief when temperatures areawide are expected to sit in the 80s. \nSome isolated showers and non-severe storms are possible (~15-30% \nchance) during the afternoon hours. \n\nActive weather makes a return Tuesday evening as a shortwave and \nrolls through the Plains. Though there is considerable uncertainty \nat this stage, a signal for ~1500 J/kg of MUCAPE and 25-30 kts of \ndeep layer shear suggests some potential for organized convection \nahead of the cold front. As the upper trough deepens this upcoming \nweek, northwesterly flow looks to enshroud the Central Plains, \npotentially introducing additional rain and storm chances through \nmid/late week.",
